  :doc/body
  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 2

## Carried forward

Iteration 1 ranked C1 second and left it untested: hoist the ten-byte
reciprocal that eight constant divisions each load identically. Its predicted
cost was three instructions of setup.

## Reflect, again, with better information

Reading the register map rather than the disassembly changed the cost estimate.
The allocator hands out four registers — RAX, RCX, RDX, R8. R9 carries the
context pointer the fuel counter lives behind. RDI and RSI are argument
registers. R10 and R11 are the division's own scratch. That leaves RBX, which
is callee-saved, so C1 as written meant reserving it, changing the prologue and
epilogue, and taking on a correctness obligation toward every caller.

**The cost was not three instructions. It was a frame-construction change.**
A candidate's cost is only knowable once you know which register is free, and
that is not in the disassembly.

## Evolve

The obstacle was that the sign correction borrowed R10:

    mov r10, r11
    shr r10, 63
    add r11, r10

RDX is dead at that point in all three numerator branches, and the pop restores
it regardless. Moving the correction onto RDX frees R10 with no prologue
change, no callee-saved obligation and no added instruction. C1' is C1 with the
blocking constraint removed rather than paid for.

## Measure

Exactly the predicted static gain:

| | before | after |
|---|---:|---:|
| bytes | 568 | 498 |
| instructions | 164 | 157 |
| `movabs` | 8 | 1 |

**And no measurable time.** 25 rotated samples under Rosetta: 14.94 min /
18.63 median against 15.10 / 18.74.

## Meta-review

- **Instruction count and latency are different fitness functions, and this
  iteration is where treating them as one broke.** Twelve percent of the
  function's bytes are gone and it takes the same time, because those eight
  loads sit outside the dependency chain and a wide out-of-order core absorbs
  them. Iteration 1 ranked candidates on instruction count as a proxy for time.
  That proxy is good for anything on the critical path and worthless for
  anything beside it, and nothing in the ranking distinguished the two.
- **Rank on the chain, not on the count.** The next ranking should ask whether
  a candidate shortens the dependency chain. The stack round trip removed
  earlier did, and it moved the clock; the reciprocal loads do not, and it did
  not.
- **Reflection improves when it reads the constraint rather than the output.**
  C1's cost was wrong until the register map was read. The same class as
  iteration 1's refutation: the disassembly says what runs, not what owns it or
  what is free.
- Both results are still worth landing. Bytes are a real budget somewhere else
  — a narrower core, or a working set under I-cache pressure — and this host is
  neither.

## Score so far

| iteration | candidate | predicted | measured |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | C2 copy propagation | −8 instructions | −1, refuted |
| 2 | C1' reciprocal hoist | −7, −70 bytes | exact, no time |

Two of two static predictions about **counts** were informative once corrected;
zero of two moved the **clock**. Everything that moved the clock this session —
narrower constants, immediate folding, the stack-free division, `lea` — did it
by shortening what the processor had to wait for.

## Next

Nothing on the current list shortens the dependency chain. The 39
register-to-register moves are allocation, which root ADR-2608179400 names as
the distance between amu and Cranelift, and which no peephole reaches."}]
